22:18:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your experience may reflect a real pattern in the teams and workplaces you’ve been in: technical roles can attract—and sometimes retain—people who prefer structured problem-solving, focused solo work, and deep interests. In meetings, that can show up as quietness, limited eye contact, discomfort with small talk, or intense enthusiasm for specialized hobbies. But it still doesn’t mean that’s simply what developers “generally are.” A few reasons: - **Selection effects:** Certain companies, specialties, and teams—especially infrastructure, gaming, security, research, or highly technical back-end work—may concentrate those traits more than others. - **Workplace norms:** If a team already communicates minimally or treats meetings as uncomfortable, new members often adapt to that culture. - **The job’s structure:** Developers are often hired and rewarded for technical output rather than polished social performance, so differences that might be masked in client-facing jobs are more visible. - **Visibility bias:** Memorable niche interests and awkward meeting moments stand out. Sociable developers or those with conventional hobbies may simply register as less distinctive. - **Different causes, similar behavior:** Avoiding eye contact or being quiet can reflect introversion, anxiety, neurodivergence, cultural communication style, fatigue, dislike of meetings, or just concentration—not one shared “developer personality.” So: it’s reasonable to say those traits may be more common in some software teams than in the population at large. It’s less accurate to treat them as defining traits of developers as a whole, or to assume you can infer someone’s abilities or personality from their job.
