22:18:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Some people may be drawn to coding partly because it offers long stretches of independent, focused work. But “coding lets people avoid social interaction” is incomplete: professional software development usually involves requirements discussions, code reviews, planning, debugging with others, documentation, stakeholder communication, and coordination across teams. So a more accurate version is: software can be attractive to people who prefer structured, technical work and less constant interpersonal interaction than, say, sales or hospitality. That can make certain traits more visible in the field. But the field is not “made of” one kind of person, and being socially reserved, awkward, or into niche hobbies isn’t a prerequisite for either entering it or succeeding in it.
